[2026-03-15T23:20:55.686Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KW6PGfqvXZ53A8TPJd2EJ8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so the API doesn't return `connection_uris` in the response. The test script's code at line 99-100:```jsconst connUri = data.connection_uris?.[0]?.connection_uriif (connUri) {\n  testDbUrl = connUri} else {  // fallback using reveal_password\n```\n\nSo it falls through to the else branch, which constructs the URL manually from the endpoint host and role password. And the password reveal returns a valid password. So the test script SHOULD construct a valid URL.\n\nBut wait - the test log says:```Test DB URL: postgresql://neondb_owner:***@ep-late-math-a617cjeu.us-west-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require\n\n\n```\n\nThis looks correct, and the schema init and seed succeed with this URL, so the connection itself works. The problem must be that `netlify dev` isn't picking up the `.env` file properly. The test output shows the webServer starting without the \"Injected .env file env vars\" message that appears when I run it manually. Let me trace through the test flow to see where the timing might be off.\n\nThe test writes the DATABASE_URL to the `.env` file, then runs playwright which starts the webServer via `netlify dev`. But what if there's already a stale `netlify dev` process running on port 8888, and since the test uses `reuseExistingServer: true`, it's reusing that old process which doesn't have the new DATABASE_URL? The test script does kill stale processes at the start, so that should prevent this, but let me verify that's actually working.\n\n```\n\njs\ntryExec('pkill -f \"netlify dev\" 2>/dev/null || true', { stdio: 'ignore' })\n```\n\nKilling stale processes...\n\nI'm realizing the real issue might be a leftover `netlify dev` process from a previous test run that didn't get properly terminated. The test script does kill processes at the start, but there's a potential race condition—the process might take a moment to actually die, and if playwright starts before that happens, `reuseExistingServer: true` will just reuse the stale server instead of starting a fresh one.\n\nLet me try a different approach and check what's actually running on port 8888 right now to see if there's something lingering there.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1655,"cache_read_input_tokens":97469,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1655,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d86e976-1032-4e20-b927-fb9bc4791925","uuid":"e02f1de3-f342-4bee-982c-c7a194d1cc3f"}
