ization and seeding. I'm wondering if there's something different about how the Netlify function is trying to reach the endpoint versus how the seed script did.\n\nLooking at the server logs, I notice that `DATABASE_URL` is defined in both `.env` and the process environment, and Netlify dev is using the process env value. But that shouldn't prevent the function from connecting. Maybe the bundled version of the Neon driver is handling the connection string differently than the direct import in the seed script.\n\nActually, I'm wondering if the real issue is timing—the seed script wakes up the endpoint, but by the time the function tries to connect 10+ seconds later, Neon might have suspended it again. Even though the suspend timeout is 60 seconds, there could be a wake-up delay that's causing the \"endpoint not found\" error. Let me add some error logging to the function to see what `DATABASE_URL` it's actually receiving, and maybe check if the endpoint is being suspended between requests. The error message is coming from Neon's proxy when it can't route to the compute endpoint—either the endpoint hostname doesn't match an active one, it was deleted, or access is denied. Since the endpoint works for seeding, the real issue is probably that the bundled function is seeing a different DATABASE_URL than what I'm passing in the spawn command. Let me verify what DATABASE_URL is actually in the process when netlify dev ignores the .env file.\n\nActually, I'm wondering if the execSync call that runs playwright is properly passing the DATABASE_URL to the webServer process...\n\nChecking the playwright execution code...\n\nSo playwright starts the webServer as a child process and it inherits the environment variables. The webServer should have the correct DATABASE_URL for the test branch. But I'm seeing that the process env does have DATABASE_URL set, yet Neon is returning \"endpoint not found\" - which is strange since the seed operation succeeded earlier.\n\nMaybe the issue 